      "content": "You could ground Benny as a Puerto Rican gang enforcer from the Bronx, whose unpolished used-car slang and rhythmic delivery convey a sudden, violent unpredictability that disarms your detective. Introduce \"Mama G,\" a matriarchal Creole underworld boss from Tudor City, whose West Indian cadence and servant-master code emphasize familial loyalty before threats, making the danger feel inherently personal rather than transactional. Finally, consider \"Slam Jamal,\" a Brooklyn-Sun cultural runner who uses colloquialisms like \"woe'elle\" to establish a street-smart identity that cuts through the formal discourse of the intellectual detective. These specific cultural markers will transform the setting from a generic backdrop into a living, breathing ecosystem where every character's history dictates their approach to power.",
